           Finerenone with Empagliflozin in Chronic Kidney Disease
                            and Type 2 Diabetes
             Rajiv Agarwal, M.D.,1,2 Jennifer B. Green, M.D.,3 Hiddo J.L. Heerspink, Ph.D.,4 Johannes F.E. Mann, M.D.,5,6
                    Janet B. McGill, M.D.,7 Amy K. Mottl, M.D.,8 Julio Rosenstock, M.D.,9 Peter Rossing, M.D.,10,11
         Muthiah Vaduganathan, M.D., M.P.H.,12 Meike Brinker, M.D.,13 Robert Edfors, M.D., Ph.D.,14 Na Li, M.D., Ph.D.,15
     Markus F. Scheerer, Ph.D.,16 Charlie Scott, M.Sc.,17 and Masaomi Nangaku, M.D., Ph.D.,18 for the CONFIDENCE Investigators*​​

                                                                      a bs t r ac t

     BACKGROUND
     Limited evidence exists to support the simultaneous initiation of sodium–glucose                           Author affiliations are listed at the end
     cotransporter-2 inhibitors and finerenone, a nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor                       of the article. Dr. Agarwal can be contact-
                                                                                                                ed at r­agarwal@​­iu​.­edu or at Richard L.
     antagonist, in persons with chronic kidney disease and type 2 diabetes.                                    Roudebush VA Medical Center, 1481 W.
                                                                                                                10th St., 111N, Indianapolis, IN 46202.
     METHODS
     We randomly assigned participants with chronic kidney disease (estimated glomerular                        *The investigators in the CONFIDENCE
                                                                                                                 trial are listed in the Supplementary Ap-
     filtration rate [eGFR], 30 to 90 ml per minute per 1.73 m2 of body-surface area), albu-                     pendix, available at NEJM.org.
     minuria (a urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 100 to ≤5000 [with albumin mea-
                                                                                                                This article was published on June 5, 2025,
     sured in milligrams and creatinine measured in grams]), and type 2 diabetes, who                           at NEJM.org.
     were already taking a renin–angiotensin system inhibitor, in a 1:1:1 ratio to receive
                                                                                                                N Engl J Med 2025;393:533-43.
     finerenone (with empagliflozin-matching placebo) at a dose of 10 or 20 mg per day,                         DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa2410659
     empagliflozin at a dose of 10 mg per day (with finerenone-matching placebo), or a                          Copyright © 2025 Massachusetts Medical Society.
     combination of finerenone and empagliflozin. The primary outcome was the relative
     change in the log-transformed mean urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio from base-                            CME
     line to 180 days. Safety was assessed.
     RESULTS
     At baseline, the urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio was similar among the partici-
     pants in the three groups; the median value was 579 (interquartile range, 292 to 1092)
     among those with available data (265 in the combination-therapy group, 258 in the
     finerenone group, and 261 participants in the empagliflozin group). At day 180, the
     reduction in the urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio with combination therapy was
     29% greater than that with finerenone alone (least-squares mean ratio of the differ-
     ence in the change from baseline, 0.71; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.61 to 0.82;
     P<0.001) and 32% greater than that with empagliflozin alone (least-squares mean ratio
     of the difference in the change from baseline, 0.68; 95% CI, 0.59 to 0.79; P<0.001).
     Neither agent, alone or in combination, led to unexpected adverse events. Symptom-
     atic hypotension, acute kidney injury, and hyperkalemia leading to drug discontinua-
     tion were uncommon.
     CONCLUSIONS
     Among persons with both chronic kidney disease and type 2 diabetes, initial therapy
     with finerenone plus empagliflozin led to a greater reduction in the urinary albumin-
     to-creatinine ratio than either treatment alone. (Funded by Bayer; CONFIDENCE
     ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT05254002.)

